  "query_preview": "/FRIDAY THROUGH MONDAY/...\nIssued at 241 AM MDT Tue Oct 27 2026\n\nFriday, our region is forecast to be mainly in a southwesterly upper-\nlevel flow with a developing trough in the Pacific Northwest (PNW). \nSunday, a second deep digging trough over the Pacific Ocean will \nmove inland. This will interact with the existing trough over the \nPNW, but models diverge on how this interaction will play out. The \ntrack and evolution of these troughs will determine our \nprecipitation chances heading into early next week. \n\nCurrently, the first trough is forecast to become more of a cut off \nlow sometime late Friday/early Saturday. This would favor more \nwidespread precipitation chances as it would generally keep our \nregion in a southwest upper-level flow with various embedded \nshortwaves being ejected across the area Friday through at least \nTuesday. Current Probability of Precipitation (PoPs) are around 20-\n60% Friday through Monday, increasing west to east. Precipitation is \nexpected to generally be showers. Instability is overall weak Friday \nwith modest lapse rates and spotty CAPE values of <500 J/kg, so a \nweak thunderstorm or two isn't out of the question if it is able to \ntap into the weak instability in the environment. \n\nMonday has the potential to be a windy day with gusts up to 50 mph \npossible for eastern Colorado. This will also depend on how the \nupper-level troughs and precipitation chances pan out, but something \nto keep an eye on. \n\nTemperature wise, Friday is expected to be pleasant with high \ntemperatures forecast in the 60s to 70s. Saturday through Monday, we \ncool slightly with highs in the 60s. Overnight Monday into \nTuesday, temperatures could approach freezing across our \nColorado counties, with the potential for our first snow of the \nseason. Confidence remains low overall due to uncertainty in the\nweekend forecast, especially given that it is 7 days out.",
